"A second objection is Aristotles. A number is not a cause. To say that justice is the number four or marriage the number five explains nothing about justice or marriage, and the heavens were given a tenth body for the sake of tidiness. Aristotles point is fair against the schools excesses. The core of the idea has outlasted it: modern physics says that the behavior of things is captured by equations, which is close to what the octave first suggested.": "d05ecef129ea",
"His followers generalized without restraint. Aristotle reports that since numbers seemed to be the first things in the whole of nature, they supposed the elements of numbers to be the elements of all things, and the whole heaven to be a musical scale and a number. The planets, moving at distances that stand in harmonic ratios, were thought to produce a music that we do not notice because we have heard it since birth. Aristotle also records the weakness of the method. Ten being the perfect number, there had to be ten heavenly bodies. Only nine could be seen, so they invented a tenth, the counter-earth. The same school thus produced the idea behind mathematical physics and an early example of fitting the facts to the theory.": "36c98da5ed32",
"Nothing written by Pythagoras survives, and the first full accounts of his life date from seven or eight centuries after his death. What follows is tradition, some of it early. He was born about 570 BCE on Samos, an island off the coast of what is now Turkey, the son of a man named Mnesarchus. Later writers send him to Egypt and Babylon to learn from the priests. When he came home he found Samos under the tyrant Polycrates, and about 530 he sailed west to Croton, a Greek city on the toe of Italy.": "1690564e61c6",
"Pythagoras is the hardest of the early philosophers to see. He wrote nothing, or nothing that lasted. His followers were sworn to secrecy and credited every discovery of the school to the master. Within a few generations he was the subject of miracle stories: he had a golden thigh, he was seen in two cities at the same hour, a river greeted him by name. The detailed Lives were written in the third century CE by authors who wanted a pagan holy man to set beside Christ.": "680c8ef5d795",
"The brotherhoods power made enemies. About 500 BCE a man who had been refused admission, or in another version the citizens of Croton fearing a tyranny, set fire to the house where the Pythagoreans were meeting. The accounts of the founders death disagree. In the best-known one he escaped the flames and ran until he came to a field of beans. He stopped, saying that he would be captured sooner than cross it, and his pursuers cut his throat. In another he reached the temple of the Muses at Metapontum and starved there after forty days.": "cd0cffae64ed",
"The communities were broken up, and the survivors scattered across the Greek world. They split into those who kept the rules and sayings and those who pursued mathematics and the study of nature. It was the second group, a century later, that impressed Plato, and through Plato the Pythagorean conviction that reality is mathematical entered the main line of European thought.": "dee7bd401c4a",
"The community lived by rule. Members held their property in common, and the saying Friends have all things in common was attributed to the founder. A newcomer kept silence for five years, listening to the masters teaching without seeing him, and was admitted to his presence only after an examination. Women were admitted as well as men, which was unusual in Greece. There were rules about food, the most famous being the ban on beans, for which ancient writers gave a dozen conflicting reasons. Teachings were handed down by word of mouth and introduced with the phrase that became a proverb in Latin: ipse dixit, he himself said it.": "4afec8305012",
"The differences go deep. For Pythagoras what is reborn is a soul, a lasting thing that passes from body to body and remains itself. The Buddha taught rebirth and denied that there is any such thing. Pythagorass followers settled questions with he himself said it. The Buddha told his hearers not to accept a teaching out of respect for the teacher, and shortly before his death he said: Ive taught the Dhamma without making any distinction between secret and public teachings. The Realized One doesnt have the closed fist of a tutor. And where the Pythagoreans found release in contemplating the eternal order of number, the Buddha found it in seeing that nothing in experience is eternal (Dharma and Western Philosophy D2, D3).": "4104c3624ed7",
"The discovery that tradition credits to Pythagoras concerns sound. Stop a stretched string at half its length and it sounds an octave higher. Stop it at two-thirds and it sounds a fifth higher, and at three-quarters a fourth. The intervals that the ear hears as concordant correspond to the ratios 2:1, 3:2, and 4:3, which use only the first four whole numbers. A quality that everyone had taken to be a matter of feeling turned out to have an exact numerical structure. It was the first law of nature to be stated in mathematical form.": "c5eacddebb78",
"The doctrine for which he was best known in his own time was the rebirth of the soul. He claimed, the story goes, to remember his former lives, among them that of a Trojan warrior named Euphorbus. His contemporary Xenophanes made fun of this in verses that are the earliest surviving reference to him. Passing a man who was beating a puppy, Pythagoras cried: Stay, smite not! Tis a friend, a human soul; I knew him straight whenas I heard him yelp! The joke is evidence. Within his lifetime he was known for teaching that souls pass between humans and animals, and for acting on it.": "bee285598d6c",
"The doctrine was refuted from within. If all things are number, and number means whole numbers and their ratios, then every length should be expressible as a ratio of whole numbers. Take a square whose side is one unit. By the theorem that carries Pythagorass name, the square on its diagonal is two. Some member of the school proved that no ratio of whole numbers, when multiplied by itself, gives exactly two. The diagonal and the side have no common measure. Later legend says that the man who made this public, Hippasus, was drowned at sea for it. Whatever happened to him, the discovery stood, and Greek mathematics was rebuilt on geometry as a result.": "d508fcc8527f",
"The earliest witnesses are few and not friendly. Xenophanes mocks him over the puppy. Heraclitus says that he practiced inquiry more than any other man and made a wisdom of his own out of much learning and poor workmanship. These lines confirm that there was such a man, that he was famous, that he taught rebirth, and that he was learned. They say nothing about mathematics. Scholars today divide over whether the historical Pythagoras was a mathematician at all, or a religious teacher whose followers later became mathematicians and gave him the credit.": "1fbcd14922d5",
"The two men were near contemporaries and never heard of each other, and the resemblances have struck readers since antiquity. Each founded a community with a rule of life, shared property, a period of probation, and places for women. Each taught that beings are reborn across the line between human and animal, and drew from this a duty of kindness to animals. Each was said to remember his own former lives. Each valued silence and self-examination. Later Greeks guessed that Pythagoras had learned from India. There is no evidence that he did, and the likeness is better explained by two reforming teachers reaching similar conclusions from the same starting point, the belief in rebirth.": "04661c03efcf",
"There he founded a community that was at once a school, a religious order, and for a time a governing party. Diogenes Laertius reports that its members, about three hundred in number, ran the city so well that its constitution was in effect a true aristocracy. Asked by a ruler what he was, Pythagoras is said to have answered, A philosopher, and to have compared life to the great games. Some come to compete and some come to sell, but the best as spectators. If the story is true, this is the first use of the word.": "b95a9cdac3a3",
